<rml>
<head>
	<title>Buff Strip</title>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="base.rcss"/>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="buff_strip.rcss"/>
</head>
<body data-model="buff_strip">
	<!-- Horizontally centered (.center-x, base.rcss) + a fixed top offset -- NOT a fixed left
	     offset "beside the MU Helper bar" (an earlier version of this file assumed that, caught
	     as wrong during verification). Derived from CNewUIBuffWindow::SetPos(int iScreenWidth)
	     (NewUISystem.cpp): its four hardcoded (iScreenWidth -> x) pairs all solve
	     x = (iScreenWidth - 200) / 2 -- true horizontal centering within the available "clear"
	     middle-of-screen width (200 = BUFF_MAX_LINE_COUNT * (BUFF_IMG_WIDTH + BUFF_IMG_SPACE), the
	     strip's own full-row content width), where iScreenWidth itself shrinks as inventory-style
	     side panels dock and eat into that space (Engine/Object/ZzzInventory.cpp's
	     GetScreenWidth()). The panel-width-reactive shrinking isn't reproduced (fixed-anchor
	     policy, docs/rmlui-ui-system/layout-and-scaling.md), but the CENTERING itself is the
	     actual layout intent and is reproduced -- .center-x behaves correctly at any resolution,
	     unlike a fixed px/dp offset would. -->
	<div id="panel" class="center-x">
		<!-- One template instance per active buff -- see BuffStrip.h's own comment: the actual
		     thing this pilot verifies is whether this data-for array correctly re-renders when
		     the bound vector's SIZE changes every frame (buffs gained/expiring), not just its
		     contents. slot_left/slot_top are pre-computed grid positions (C++ side, BuffStrip.cpp
		     SyncRmlModel()) -- no arithmetic done here, same "push already-computed pixel values"
		     pattern char_make.rml's job buttons already use.

		     Bound with + 'dp', NOT + 'px' like that job-button precedent -- confirmed by direct
		     in-game testing that the two are NOT interchangeable here: RmlUi's `dp` unit is scaled
		     by Context::SetDensityIndependentPixelRatio() (RmlUiRuntime.cpp's ApplyUIScale(),
		     driven by GameConfig's user-configurable UI scale), while `px` is always literal,
		     unscaled pixels (RmlUi/Source/Core/ComputeProperty.cpp). buff-slot/buff-icon-art's own
		     sizes are declared in `dp` (buff_strip.rcss), so at any UI scale other than 100% a
		     `px`-based offset drifts out of step with them -- visible here as icons with no gap
		     between them at all (BUFF_IMG_SPACE compressed away) despite the same C++-computed
		     pixel math job buttons use successfully. This file's own grid math is unitless by
		     design (BuffStrip.cpp) specifically so the RML side can pick whichever unit its own
		     sibling CSS actually uses -- job buttons' panel/buttons are all `px`-sized in
		     char_make.rcss, this strip's are all `dp`-sized, hence the difference here. -->
		<div class="buff-slot" data-for="buff : buffs"
		     data-style-left="buff.slot_left + 'dp'" data-style-top="buff.slot_top + 'dp'">
			<!-- decorator is a full pre-built value, e.g. "image(atlas1-23)" (BuffStrip.cpp's
			     BuildIconDecorator(), see BuffEntry::decorator's comment in BuffStrip.h for why
			     this replaced an earlier, confirmed-non-working clipped-oversized-image attempt)
			     -- picks one of the named @spritesheet rects declared in buff_strip.rcss. -->
			<div class="buff-icon-art" data-style-decorator="buff.decorator"></div>
			<span class="tooltip">{{buff.tooltip}}</span>
		</div>
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
